<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Bryansk</h1><p class="st-meta">1246 CE – 1356 CE (approx.) · capital Bryansk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A forest principality on the Desna that inherited the senior role in the Chernigov lands after the Mongol sack, before falling to Lithuania.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryansk">Bryansk</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desna-river">Desna</a> was a town of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-chernigov">Principality of Chernigov</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> in 1239 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-chernigov">Prince Mikhail</a> was killed in 1246, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-mikhailovich-of-bryansk">Roman Mikhailovich</a> made Bryansk his seat, and it became the leading principality of the old Chernigov lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Protected by thick forests, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryansk">Bryansk</a> recovered more quickly than the open lands to the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-mikhailovich-of-bryansk">Roman Mikhailovich</a>, who ruled until the 1280s, fought off Lithuanian raids and allied by marriage with the princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Galicia-Volhynia</a>. From the early 14th century princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> line held the town, and it was torn by disputes between pro-Lithuanian and pro-Horde factions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1356 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryansk">Bryansk</a> and later gave it to his son Dmitry. It remained Lithuanian until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> took it in 1500.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1246 CE – 1356 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.50%;width:2.19%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1246 CE – 1356 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bryansk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Roman Mikhailovich, Dmitry Olgerdovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Briansk Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1301&amp;focus=principality-of-bryansk" aria-label="Principality of Bryansk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5446 346 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Bryansk lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5766 602L5724 651M5766 602L6352 637M5766 602L5556 562"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5724 651h0M6352 637h0M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5766 602h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5766 602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bryansk. <a href="/atlas?year=1301&amp;focus=principality-of-bryansk">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
